Per-State Signatures and Mean Time-to-Failure: An Algorithm for Systems With Simultaneous Failures Skip to main content Report GitHub Issue × Title: Content selection saved. Describe the issue below: Description: Why HTML? Report Issue Back to Abstract Download PDF Contents Reading mode Colour theme License: arXiv.org perpetual non-exclusive license arXiv:2610.22181v1 [cs.DS] 15 Oct 2026 Per-State Signatures and Mean Time-to-Failure: An Algorithm for Systems With Simultaneous Failures Guido Lagos Simón Ávila † † thanks: This work was supported by the grants ANID/FONDECYT/Iniciación˜11230256 and ANID/FONDECYT/Regular˜1231207. (Corresponding author: Guido Lagos.) † † thanks: G.˜Lagos and S.˜Ávila are with Universidad Adolfo Ibáñez, Viña del Mar, Chile (e-mail: guido.lagos@uai.cl; simoavila@alumnos.uai.cl). Abstract We present an algorithm that computes the structural signature and the mean time-to-failure (MTTF) of a monotone binary system at every one of its states, rather than only at the state in which all components work. It makes no assumption on the structure function and evaluates it exactly once per state, as many times as needed for the signature of the complete system alone, so computing the MTTF everywhere costs only a factor polynomial in the number of components over computing it once. As a by-product, it identifies all minimal path sets. We assume exchangeable component failure times whose survivor count is a continuous-time Markov chain. This setting admits simultaneous failures, as in the Lévy-frailty Marshall-Olkin, binomial failure rate and lethal-shock models, where the simplifications available for single failures do not apply. We use the per-state MTTF to define threshold repair policies. We show that the per-state signature is a strictly finer decision criterion than the MTTF: signature dominance at a common failure count implies MTTF dominance, but not conversely. Under the Lévy-frailty Marshall-Olkin model the MTTF is monotone in the state, which yields the long-run cost rate of every threshold policy in a single pass. Experiments on 22 benchmark networks with up to 30 components and 31 failure models show that the best MTTF-threshold policy has a median excess cost of 0.36% over the optimal state-based policy, and that it is never worse than the best policy that repairs after a fixed number of failures. Index Terms: System signature, mean time-to-failure, coherent system, exchangeable failure times, simultaneous failures, common-cause failure, Marshall-Olkin distribution, condition-based maintenance, repair policy. I Introduction Reliability indices of a multi-component system are almost always reported for the system with every component working. Maintenance decisions, however, are taken at partially failed states: an operator observes which components have failed, and must decide whether to keep operating or to intervene. Two states with the same number of failed components can differ sharply in how much useful life remains, depending on which components failed, and a decision rule that ignores this distinction discards information that is available for free. Acting on it requires the reliability index not at one state, but at all of them. In this work we provide an algorithm that computes the structural signature and the mean time-to-failure (MTTF) at each of the 2 n 2^{n} states of a monotone binary system with n n components, at essentially the cost of computing them once. I-A Setting and notation We consider a system with n > 1 n>1 components, each of which is either working or failed . We represent a configuration of the system by a state 𝐱 \mathbf{x} in { 0 , 1 } n \{0,1\}^{n} , where x i = 1 x_{i}=1 if component i i works and x i = 0 x_{i}=0 if it has failed, and we write | 𝐱 | := ∑ i = 1 n x i |\mathbf{x}|:=\sum_{i=1}^{n}x_{i} for the number of working components. A deterministic and known structure function Φ : { 0 , 1 } n → { 0 , 1 } \Phi:\{0,1\}^{n}	o\{0,1\} gives the status of the system, with Φ ⁡ ( 𝐱 ) = 1 \Phi(\mathbf{x})=1 when the system works and 0 0 otherwise. The system is monotone , meaning that Φ ⁡ ( 𝐱 1 ) ≤ Φ ⁡ ( 𝐱 2 ) \Phi(\mathbf{x}^{1})\leq\Phi(\mathbf{x}^{2}) whenever 𝐱 1 ≤ 𝐱 2 \mathbf{x}^{1}\leq\mathbf{x}^{2} componentwise: a failed system cannot be made to work by breaking down working components, and a working system cannot be made to fail by repairing failed ones. We also assume the system is non-trivial, i.e., that Φ \Phi is neither identically 0 0 nor identically 1 1 ; by monotonicity this is equivalent to Φ ⁡ ( 𝟎 ) = 0 \Phi(\mathbf{0})=0 and Φ ⁡ ( 𝟏 ) = 1 \Phi(\mathbf{1})=1 , where 𝟎 = ( 0 , … , 0 ) 𝖳 \mathbf{0}=(0,\ldots,0)^{\mathsf{T}} and 𝟏 = ( 1 , … , 1 ) 𝖳 \mathbf{1}=(1,\ldots,1)^{\mathsf{T}} . We do not require every component to be relevant, so semi-coherent systems are covered. The structural signature of the system [ 1 , 2 , 3 ] is the vector 𝐬 \mathbf{s} in { 0 , 1 } n \{0,1\}^{n} given by s k := S ¯ k − 1 − S ¯ k for ​ k = 1 , … , n \displaystyle s_{k}:= ar{S}_{k-1}- ar{S}_{k}\qquad	ext{for }k=1,\ldots,n (1) with 𝐒 ¯  ar{\mathbf{S}} in { 0 , 1 } n + 1 \{0,1\}^{n+1} the tail signature vector [ 4 ] 1 1 1 A related concept is the sequence S ¯ n − l  ar{S}_{n-l} for l = 1 , … , n l=1,\ldots,n , where S ¯ k  ar{S}_{k} is as defined in ( 2 ), first defined in [ 5 ] and then called survival signature in [ 6 ] . given by S ¯ k := W k W k + F k = W k ( n k ) for ​ k = 0 , … , n , \displaystyle ar{S}_{k}:= rac{W_{k}}{W_{k}+F_{k}}= rac{W_{k}}{ inom{n}{k}}\qquad	ext{for }k=0,\ldots,n, (2) where W k W_{k} and F k F_{k} count the states with k k failed components in which the system works, respectively fails. That is, W k := \displaystyle W_{k}:= ∑ 𝐱 : | 𝐱 | = n − k Φ ( 𝐱 ) \displaystyle\sum_{\mathbf{x}\,:\,|\mathbf{x}|=n-k}\Phi(\mathbf{x}) (3) and F k := ∑ 𝐱 : | 𝐱 | = n − k ( 1 − Φ ( 𝐱 ) ) F_{k}:=\sum_{\mathbf{x}\,:\,|\mathbf{x}|=n-k}(1-\Phi(\mathbf{x})) , with W k + F k = ( n k ) W_{k}+F_{k}= inom{n}{k} . Thus, S ¯ k  ar{S}_{k} is 