ilable for single failures break down. We delimit the resulting model class precisely, and illustrate it with several families of failure models, including the Lévy-frailty Marshall-Olkin family and the binomial failure rate and lethal-shock models of probabilistic safety assessment. 3. As a corollary, the same sweep identifies all minimal path sets of the system, at no additional cost in evaluations of Φ \Phi . 4. We use the per-state MTTF to define threshold repair policies, and compare them through their long-run expected cost rate. We further show (Theorem 5 ) that signature dominance at a common failure count implies MTTF dominance, and that the MTTF is a single linear projection of the per-state signature; consequently, signature-threshold policies strictly refine MTTF-threshold policies. Under the Lévy-frailty Marshall-Olkin distribution the MTTF is monotone in the state (Lemma 4 ), and we show that this yields the long-run cost of all MTTF-threshold policies in a single pass over the states, grouped into classes with the same counting vector (Proposition 3 ). All costs include a setup cost per repair intervention. We also compute the optimal state-based policy, which bounds what any policy based on the per-state signature can achieve. 5. We report computational experiments on 22 benchmark networks with up to 30 components, 31 failure models calibrated to a common pairwise beta factor, and 45 cost settings (Section VII ). The best MTTF-threshold policy has a median excess cost of 0.36 % 0.36\% over the optimal state-based policy, and it is never worse than the best r r -out-of- n n policy, over which it saves a median of 4.2 % 4.2\% ; its cost curve, however, is neither convex nor unimodal in the threshold, i.e., it can increase and then decrease again, so the threshold must be tuned over all breakpoints. I-D Organization Section II reviews related work. Section III states our assumptions on the failure-time distribution and formulates the per-state MTTF. Section IV presents the recursion, the algorithm and its complexity analysis. Section V develops the MTTF-threshold repair policies and their long-run cost, and the optimal state-based policy. Section VI establishes the dominance and expressiveness results. Section VII reports the computational experiments, and Section VIII concludes. The proofs of all results are in Appendix A . II Related work Signatures and their computation Since its introduction by Samaniego [ 1 ] , the signature has become a standard tool for comparing and evaluating coherent systems [ 2 , 3 ] : stochastic orders between systems follow from orders between their signatures [ 10 ] , and the decomposition ( 4 ) extends from iid to exchangeable lifetimes [ 8 ] , the setting we adopt. Marichal et al. [ 7 ] gave the counting form ( 2 ), and Coolen and Coolen-Maturi [ 6 ] the survival signature for several component types. Computing the signature exactly is the practical bottleneck. Existing methods enumerate minimal path or cut sets [ 11 , 12 , 13 ] , or use symbolic representations of the structure function such as binary decision diagrams [ 14 ] , and they target the signature of the complete system. We need one signature per state, i.e., 2 n 2^{n} of them, and we are not aware of an algorithm that produces these, for an arbitrary structure function, at a cost comparable to a single signature computation. Reliability of used and partially failed systems Mixture representations of the residual lifetime of a used system [ 15 ] , and of its conditional residual life given the number of failures and the system status [ 16 ] , condition on the number of failed components rather than on their identity. They are representations rather than algorithms: obtaining the value at every state from them would repeat the derivation state by state, without exploiting that neighboring states share almost all of their sub-states, which is what Lemma 1 does. Exchangeable models with a Markov survivor count Ross, Shahshahani and Weiss [ 17 ] identified the structure on which Assumptions (A)–(B) of Section III rest; the model of Ross [ 18 ] , in its exchangeable case, and sequential order statistics with exponential baseline [ 19 ] are single-failure instances. Together, (A) and (B) force a pure-death chain on the number of working components, which excludes models based on Markovian arrival processes [ 20 , 21 ] , semi-Markov multi-state models [ 22 ] and exchangeable dependent-renewal constructions [ 23 ] ; among the models they admit, the Lévy-frailty Marshall-Olkin family is the richest tractable one with simultaneous failures. Simultaneous and common-cause failures The multivariate exponential distribution of Marshall and Olkin [ 24 ] is the canonical model of common shocks; its specializations in probabilistic safety assessment are the binomial failure rate [ 25 , 26 ] and lethal-shock [ 26 ] models. The Lévy-frailty Marshall-Olkin family [ 27 ] is its exchangeable subclass driven by a Lévy subordinator, whose order statistics and asymptotics were analyzed in [ 28 , 29 ] , and repair policies and decompositions in [ 30 ] . We note in Section VII-A that the binomial failure rate and lethal-shock models are of Lévy-frailty type, so this family covers the standard common-cause models. Maintenance driven by reliability indices and by signatures Signature-based maintenance is surveyed in [ 31 ] . Finkelstein et al. [ 32 ] proposed “time-free” policies triggered by the number of failed components; in these and related policies the trigger is a scalar index, and the signature enters only through the expected costs and cycle lengths. Closest to our setting, Feng and Yun [ 33 ] replace all failed components once the mean residual life of the system drops below a tuned threshold; Section V discusses why their computation is not viable beyond very small systems. III MTTF at all states We focus on computing the signature of the subsystem obtained at each state 𝐱 \mathbf{x} in { 0 , 1 } n 